1743,EQ ID 1743,PMU 2 Spotwelder KVA-30 (#11789),Assembly,2561,10/12/2022,"3. Prod Affected, Not Stopped",Oil leak inside the machine,Closed Without Action,0,0.5,Preston,Maintenance,0,03/01/2023 10:30 AM,"Preston Ellexson - 03/01/23  8:21:39 AM: This is not an emergency exit and shouldn't be used as one. We have this door automatically locking in case the compressor room doesn't get locked at the end of the shifts. If we do this, someone could break into the building will ease after hours and nobody would know. | Preston Ellexson - 10/12/22  8:03:56 AM: The oil drip was set pretty high. I adjusted it down almost all the way. Opened the machine to inspect. The muffler is exhausting oil a bit more than it should. The valves are functioning properly while spotwelding. Cleaned up the oil and put the cover back on. I will check at the end of the week to see if we've accumulated anymore oil leaks. I am awaiting a quote for the valves.",[]
1743,EQ ID 1743,PMU 2 Spotwelder KVA-30 (#11789),Assembly,10198,02/01/2024,"5. Prod Ongoing, Maint Required",leaking air in cylinder valve and leaking at air shut off switch.,Closed and Completed,0,0.5,Josh Patton,Maintenance,0,04/08/2024 12:00 AM,Joshua Patton - 04/08/24  6:59:08 AM: replaced the Qd on the supply line,[]
1743,EQ ID 1743,PMU 2 Spotwelder KVA-30 (#11789),Assembly,15177,03/26/2025,2. General Maint - High Priority,welder stopped working,Closed and Completed,0,8,Preston,Maintenance,0,04/24/2025 10:00 AM,"Preston Ellexson - 04/24/25  8:29:43 AM: David came out 4/7 to inspect welder. We hooked up 120V to the high lead inside the electrical cabinet. Went through the current control settings to test the transformer. Resistance is showing what is needed with no issues. Transformer is in good shape. Just got this machine looked at since then. Hooked up single phase 220V to the disconnect switch. Found another current lead hit the arm when retracting. Turned all power off at the breaker. Had to move all the current control leads inside the machine so they weren't touching the top arm inside the machine. Taped over bare spot. Made sure I could put my fingers between all the leads and the arm. Nothing is close to touching now. Turned power on and tested. Did a clear all on the machine due to errors flashing when turning on. Tested current settings 2-6 with no issues. I did have to adjust the schedule due to the fact I'm not using the wild leg for a single phase, just using 120V on both. Everything welds good with minor adjustments to weld current and percent on the schedule. Tightened loose fittings and foot pedal as well. Machine is good to run. I will leave in maintenance as a backup. I am replacing the wood underneath and then it's good to run if needed. | Preston Ellexson - 03/28/25 12:24:21 PM: Tested machine after checking all other current lead wiring. When it arced out, it blew something on the current control. Tried settings 2-5 each and nothing will weld. Swapped welder with one of the backups we had. Had to do small repairs on it. Cut new wood for spacing up approx 6"". Nailed all together. Swapped regulator and gauge. It runs good, but I have the air pressure pretty high on the retract or it won't function. It's running around 80psi instead of 20-30. It's either still a bad regulator, or a bad valve. Machine runs good so I'm not going to swap anything else right now. Did the machine safety analysis and everything passed. Good to use for production. I will get with David@Spotwelding Consultants 3/31 to see if it's worth repairing. It is one of our newest machines. Leaving in pending until I figure out what we'll do with the machine. | Preston Ellexson - 03/26/25  8:15:19 AM: Work in process. Locked out until I return tomorrow. Matt was ok with that. I'll swap the welder out with an old one first thing in the morning. Current control lead arced out to ground inside machine.",[]
